  "production_date": "probably about 1495",
  "brief_description": "Saint John the Baptist is shown between Saint Zeno, the fourth-century Bishop of Verona, and a female saint. Because of the finery of her clothing and her long flowing hair, she may be Saint Catherine, who was a princess from Alexandria. However, the saint is usually shown with the spiked wheel used in her torture, so it is difficult to be sure.\n\nSaint John carries a fine reed cross, a symbol of Christ's crucifixion. The scroll curled around it is inscribed in Latin with the words he spoke about Christ when he was preaching in the wilderness (John 1: 29, 36): ECCE AGNVS ('Behold the Lamb').\n\nThe picture is in poor condition, partly because the paint surface has been transferred from the wooden panel on which it was painted to canvas.",
